# Agent shims: the one list of commands Tether wraps for agent panes.
#
# Each line is: <command> <action> <argument...>. The installer links
# ~/.local/lib/tether/shims/<command> to tether-shim; Tether puts that
# directory first on PATH in every session it creates. Each shim runs the real
# binary (the next one on PATH) with argv changed as the action says.
#
#   prepend           <flag>                  put <flag> before the arguments
#                                             (systemd-run treats anything after
#                                             its COMMAND as that command's own)
#   after-subcommand  <flag> <sub,sub,...>    put <flag> after the first argument
#                                             when it is one of the listed
#                                             subcommands (udisksctl options are
#                                             per subcommand)
#   refuse            <message>               print the message, exit 126
#   refuse-args       <word,word,...> <message>
#                                             the same, when the arguments hold
#                                             these words in this order; a name
#                                             may have several such lines
#
# Why: a system-bus polkit check from an agent process otherwise falls back to
# the owner's desktop session and pops a password dialog. With
# --no-ask-password / --no-user-interaction the call fails at once instead.
# resolvectl has no such flag, so it is not listed.
systemctl      prepend           --no-ask-password
systemd-run    prepend           --no-ask-password
systemd-mount  prepend           --no-ask-password
loginctl       prepend           --no-ask-password
timedatectl    prepend           --no-ask-password
hostnamectl    prepend           --no-ask-password
localectl      prepend           --no-ask-password
machinectl     prepend           --no-ask-password
udisksctl      after-subcommand  --no-user-interaction  mount,unmount,unlock,lock,loop-setup,loop-delete,power-off,smart-simulate
pkexec         refuse            pkexec is blocked for agents; use sudo
#
# Commands whose job is to print a stored credential. Whatever an agent prints
# lands in its transcript and goes to its model provider, so these never run
# from an agent pane (2026-10-27: `git credential fill` in a research probe
# printed the owner's GitHub token). git and gh otherwise run unchanged; git's
# own credential helper still answers git push/fetch.
git            refuse-args       credential,fill             git credential fill prints stored credentials; blocked for agents
git            refuse-args       credential-store,get        git credential-store get prints stored credentials; blocked for agents
git            refuse-args       credential-cache,get        git credential-cache get prints stored credentials; blocked for agents
gh             refuse-args       auth,token                  gh auth token prints the GitHub token; blocked for agents
gh             refuse-args       auth,git-credential         gh auth git-credential prints the GitHub token; blocked for agents
gh             refuse-args       auth,status,--show-token    gh auth status --show-token prints the GitHub token; blocked for agents
gh             refuse-args       auth,status,-t              gh auth status -t prints the GitHub token; blocked for agents
secret-tool    refuse-args       lookup                      secret-tool lookup prints a stored secret; blocked for agents
